#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087454 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a6128-22df-5a02-96fd-7c8cabf8f5f6" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Complex Number in Polar Form"}] :question/id #uuid "b4055943-40a7-5920-9850-a9f1f50da9c8" :question/math-academy-id "e-5089" :question/problem "Express $z=-5+12\\textrm{i}$ in the form $r(\\cos\\theta + \\textrm{i}\\sin\\theta)$. Round $\\theta$ to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "In polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $\\mid z \\mid$. For a complex number\n\n$$\nz=x+y\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nthe magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mid z \\mid = \\sqrt{x^2+y^2}\n$$\n\nSo, for\n\n$$\nz = -5+12\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nthe magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr = \\mid z \\mid &= \\sqrt{(-5)^{2} + (12)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{25 + 144} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{169} \\\\\n&= 13\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta$ represents the argument $\\arg(z)$. Since $x = -5$ is negative and $y = 12$ is positive, $z$ lies in the second quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ = \\arg (z) &= π - \\arctan \\mid \\frac{y}{x} \\mid \\\\\n&= π - \\arctan \\mid \\frac{12}{-5} \\mid \\\\\n&= π - \\arctan (\\frac{12}{5}) \\\\\n&\\approx 1.966\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nFinally, we write the complex number\n\n$$\nz = -5 + 12\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nin polar form:\n\n$$\nz = 13(\\cos(1.966)+\\textrm{i}\\sin(1.966))\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087455 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31e4455a-9f8f-5c12-8c96-bb6735691591" :knowledge-point/title "Converting From Polar Form to Cartesian Form"}] :question/id #uuid "e50fe1bc-2a1d-5913-b9d7-013c05baf246" :question/math-academy-id "e-5090" :question/problem "Express $z=4\\left(\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right)+\\textrm{i}\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right)\\right)$ in the form $z=x+\\textrm{i}y$." :question/worked-solution "We will simplify the right-hand side. Since\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right) = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz &= 4(\\cos (\\frac{5π}{6}) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (\\frac{5π}{6})) \\\\\n&= 4(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} + \\textrm{i}\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\\n&=-2\\sqrt{3} + 2\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087456 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d1ea5c4-2700-5c03-a22e-de5439f47c36" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Quotient of Complex Numbers in Polar Form"}] :question/id #uuid "3b103330-6aa7-5f1a-9a83-808515a43f97" :question/math-academy-id "e-5091" :question/problem "Express $z=\\dfrac{3}{2-\\textrm{i}}$ in the form $r(\\cos\\theta+\\textrm{i}\\sin\\theta)$." :question/worked-solution "First, we express the given complex number in the form\n\n$$\nz=x+y\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nTo do this, we remove $\\textrm{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $2+\\textrm{i}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz &= \\frac{3}{2 - \\textrm{i}} \\\\\n&= (3(2 + \\textrm{i}))/((2 - \\textrm{i})(2 + \\textrm{i})) \\\\\n&= (3(2 + \\textrm{i}))/(2^{2} + 1^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{5}(2 + \\textrm{i}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{6}{5} + \\frac{3}{5}\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $\\mid z \\mid$. For a complex number\n\n$$\nz=x+y\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nthe magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mid z \\mid = \\sqrt{x^2+y^2}\n$$\n\nSo, for\n\n$$\nz= \\dfrac{6}{5} +\\dfrac{3}{5}\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nthe magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr = \\mid z \\mid &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{6}{5})^{2} + (\\frac{3}{5})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{36 + 9}{25}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{45}{25}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta$ represents the argument $\\arg(z)$. Since\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{6}{5}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nare both positive, $z$ lies in the first quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\arg (z) &= \\arctan (((\\frac{3}{5}))/((\\frac{6}{5}))) \\\\\n&= \\arctan (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\\n&= 0.464\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz = \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\left (\\cos(0.464)+ \\textrm{i} \\sin(0.464) \\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265945 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a6128-22df-5a02-96fd-7c8cabf8f5f6" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Complex Number in Polar Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c290a84a-2604-5ba5-b7ac-2cd67cd802da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265947} {:answer/id #uuid "75ea7e4b-dee0-5acb-8da9-102ec8dba84e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265948} {:answer/id #uuid "d02dc53c-5a99-59f8-b1c1-a33b49c094e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265949} {:answer/id #uuid "bc6cf462-d1ff-5367-8640-e982e089afea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265950} {:answer/id #uuid "80677a3e-179c-5307-8815-5716e23a8b1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75ea7e4b-dee0-5acb-8da9-102ec8dba84e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265948} :answer-field/id #uuid "c33d0329-b852-5c2b-b2a2-03f0bcefea5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265946}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d74f8b68-3c0c-59b0-82f5-fe441f002de9" :question/math-academy-id "q-107402" :question/problem "Express $z=-2\\text{i}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ We get\n\n$$\nz=0+(-2)\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=0+(-2)\\text{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{(0)}^{2}+{(-2)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n      \n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=0$  and $y=-2$ is negative, $z$ lies on the negative $y$-axis. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z=-2\\text{i}$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}))\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265952 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a6128-22df-5a02-96fd-7c8cabf8f5f6" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Complex Number in Polar Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37fe3d7e-4b95-5d99-b141-cd7c35573b18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265954} {:answer/id #uuid "4e63922c-9952-5728-840a-ddb17d6091b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265955} {:answer/id #uuid "c3176ea4-f443-50f2-8b30-b3818482e106" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265956} {:answer/id #uuid "47635084-efa7-5caa-8f5a-79257e4932d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265957} {:answer/id #uuid "eda9688d-579f-5fa8-85b7-ca355922a5c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37fe3d7e-4b95-5d99-b141-cd7c35573b18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265954} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5b8cd4e-2ca5-5f25-83bd-999a34e1c748" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265953}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6a55cea2-0632-5c86-ad59-90081e2751a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-107404" :question/problem "Express $z=\\frac{1-\\text{i}}{4}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ We get\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{1-\\text{i}}{4}=\\frac{1}{4}+(-\\frac{1}{4})\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=\\frac{1}{4}+(-\\frac{1}{4})\\text{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n     \n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ is positive and $y=-\\frac{1}{4}$ is negative, $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{4})}{(\\frac{1}{4})}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1 \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z=\\frac{1-\\text{i}}{4}$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}))\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265959 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d1ea5c4-2700-5c03-a22e-de5439f47c36" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Quotient of Complex Numbers in Polar Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79662cb1-82d2-5532-aa08-286b903c8ddf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{2}{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265961} {:answer/id #uuid "c42f919a-0c91-5215-8bfb-970af4dd5aa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{4}{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265962} {:answer/id #uuid "df65c59e-9e0d-50e5-a29f-22ee643f6a97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{2}{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265963} {:answer/id #uuid "a79c9618-9d26-5d61-bd73-ff3ad0033cdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{2}{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265964} {:answer/id #uuid "f35b0eec-3c71-5b02-a35e-42d4423a20fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{4}{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265965}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79662cb1-82d2-5532-aa08-286b903c8ddf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{2}{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265961} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e588cbb-f092-53bf-8fa5-011809180929" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265960}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c0791cfa-321a-5888-9413-89be1eb2fcca" :question/math-academy-id "q-107504" :question/problem "Express $z=-\\frac{2}{3\\text{i}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $-3\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =-\\frac{2}{3\\text{i}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2(-3\\text{i})}{(3\\text{i})(-3\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\text{i}}{-9{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\text{i}}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=\\frac{2}{3}\\text{i}=0+\\frac{2}{3}\\text{i},$ the magnitude is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=0$  and $y=\\frac{2}{3}$ is positive, $z$ lies on the positive direction of the $y$-axis. So,  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{2}{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265966 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31e4455a-9f8f-5c12-8c96-bb6735691591" :knowledge-point/title "Converting From Polar Form to Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7db9aa1-c83b-5b28-8656-36516e884448" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=1+\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265968} {:answer/id #uuid "d8958a6d-d3f7-5d44-a000-22abc44a95f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2\\sqrt{2}+2\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265969} {:answer/id #uuid "efa77ae0-ab0d-5a05-9007-74c4a383ff7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{2}+2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265970} {:answer/id #uuid "9fd6a82f-821e-5378-9b9b-38455ff56ea9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2\\sqrt{2}+\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265971} {:answer/id #uuid "8a471d1d-11d6-5c18-9e9e-406274bcde82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=1+2\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265972}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8958a6d-d3f7-5d44-a000-22abc44a95f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2\\sqrt{2}+2\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265969} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd565e33-d99b-5f3b-841e-80d207e6c0ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265967}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bf55f526-0929-5f91-84c0-2f0248a9d9bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-17162" :question/problem "Express $z=4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))$ in the form $z=x+\\text{i}y.$" :question/worked-solution "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\text{i}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}+2\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265973 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31e4455a-9f8f-5c12-8c96-bb6735691591" :knowledge-point/title "Converting From Polar Form to Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81d5b7b8-1e23-5286-8b92-58f13aa4a4bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265975} {:answer/id #uuid "aa47ac83-c599-5d64-af74-0ce01009fe9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa47ac83-c599-5d64-af74-0ce01009fe9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265976} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d289dd6-ff07-598b-b6e7-32365c0d6cd7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265974} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5abc8bf4-c3af-5169-a683-057544f14d2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265978}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5abc8bf4-c3af-5169-a683-057544f14d2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265978} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3b92e66-b403-5d76-a47a-0a5f6c402ef0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265977}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b285beda-2f51-532f-8359-ee290ff727d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-321306" :question/problem "Express $z=14(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}))$ in the form $z=x+y\\,\\text{i},$ where $x$ and $y$ are real numbers. Give the **exact** values of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-$\n{{field-2}}\n$i$" :question/worked-solution "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nFirst, we find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the values and expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =14(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})) \\\\  & =14(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-\\text{i}\\,\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{14\\sqrt{3}}{2}-\\text{i}\\,\\frac{14}{2} \\\\  & =7\\sqrt{3}-7\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265979 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31e4455a-9f8f-5c12-8c96-bb6735691591" :knowledge-point/title "Converting From Polar Form to Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de5b5483-73b3-5573-ac1e-7366ffa3d670" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265981}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de5b5483-73b3-5573-ac1e-7366ffa3d670" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265981} :answer-field/id #uuid "428114ef-f99b-5a0c-bd59-0a50139b6ed2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265980} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "084e2a44-0684-54ed-a68d-1ba9e0d5c955" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265983}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "084e2a44-0684-54ed-a68d-1ba9e0d5c955" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265983} :answer-field/id #uuid "812b51ab-1051-52ae-8a2b-4f6b21a262fc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265982}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "14c208bb-1cd0-520c-8ca7-4565597bcd35" :question/math-academy-id "q-321308" :question/problem "Express $z=18(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}))$ in the form $z=x+y\\,\\text{i},$ where $x$ and $y$ are real numbers. Give the **exact** values of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+$\n{{field-2}}\n$i$" :question/worked-solution "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nFirst, we find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the values and expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =18(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})) \\\\  & =18(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\text{i}\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{18}{2}+\\text{i}\\,\\frac{18\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =-9+9\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265984 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31e4455a-9f8f-5c12-8c96-bb6735691591" :knowledge-point/title "Converting From Polar Form to Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de6b2d44-4e84-5d60-9079-c0c3a9cbbec7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265986} {:answer/id #uuid "65379b43-adeb-5a46-91aa-b7a24c3ee185" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265987}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de6b2d44-4e84-5d60-9079-c0c3a9cbbec7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265986} :answer-field/id #uuid "642dca7e-c704-5b7e-898b-d0c07d9280ca" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265985} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7ff2e97-e42d-5a34-920c-f966a8dd273e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265989}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7ff2e97-e42d-5a34-920c-f966a8dd273e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265989} :answer-field/id #uuid "46a6548d-25e4-590d-9dca-18b57f6c2488" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265988}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "04f91f4c-9cd9-5bbc-b70f-54eae6ac9ed0" :question/math-academy-id "q-321309" :question/problem "Express $z=20(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}))$ in the form $z=x+y\\,\\text{i},$ where $x$ and $y$ are real numbers. Give the **exact** values of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+$\n{{field-2}}\n$i$" :question/worked-solution "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nFirst, we find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the values and expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =20(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})) \\\\  & =20(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\text{i}\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{20\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\text{i}\\,\\frac{20\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-10\\sqrt{2}+10\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265990 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d1ea5c4-2700-5c03-a22e-de5439f47c36" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Quotient of Complex Numbers in Polar Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f6b0b43-3aa0-5a9f-8e41-d32abc3440f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265992}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f6b0b43-3aa0-5a9f-8e41-d32abc3440f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265992} :answer-field/id #uuid "002e8fa8-e9da-5939-86d8-da7a025abab4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265991} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f085ef27-3b20-5223-84d6-b64ed58a461f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265994}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f085ef27-3b20-5223-84d6-b64ed58a461f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265994} :answer-field/id #uuid "22bbd87f-fdc4-59ff-8f2d-69ce3ba0e451" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265993} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf9cc000-d0fc-5e90-832c-63299bc9d485" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265996}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf9cc000-d0fc-5e90-832c-63299bc9d485" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265996} :answer-field/id #uuid "7385871f-7cdf-55f8-8b65-df5356f87329" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265995}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a15e3b43-5e5e-572b-a4ef-754d820846a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-321383" :question/problem "Express $z=\\frac{4}{1+\\text{i}\\sqrt{3}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$ Give the **exact** values of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ in **radians** from $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(${{field-2}}$)+i\\operatorname{sin}⁡(${{field-3}}$))$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $1-\\text{i}\\sqrt{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{4}{1+\\text{i}\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1-\\text{i}\\sqrt{3})}{(1+\\text{i}\\sqrt{3})(1-\\text{i}\\sqrt{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-4\\text{i}\\sqrt{3}}{{1}^{2}-{(\\text{i}\\sqrt{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-4\\text{i}\\sqrt{3}}{1-(-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-4i\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\\\  & =1-\\sqrt{3}\\,i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=1-\\sqrt{3}\\,i,$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=1$ and $y=-\\sqrt{3},$ $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=2\\cdot \\,(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}))\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265997 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d1ea5c4-2700-5c03-a22e-de5439f47c36" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Quotient of Complex Numbers in Polar Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc85c43c-6e60-58d9-92cf-ac6b866cbd04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc85c43c-6e60-58d9-92cf-ac6b866cbd04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265999} :answer-field/id #uuid "23abaa5c-f7c3-522a-b38a-bd916814fcda" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265998} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce211202-f488-5b66-b236-b347c5372f07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce211202-f488-5b66-b236-b347c5372f07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266001} :answer-field/id #uuid "ede24cf6-2533-555c-9c03-dd872dc4268b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266000} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ebca2134-f62d-594a-abe2-3a5853b396c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebca2134-f62d-594a-abe2-3a5853b396c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266003} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7b65906-25ce-5d9d-b427-3a70d48b6883" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266002}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "752642e1-ccd9-52f8-95c0-e1145873311a" :question/math-academy-id "q-321420" :question/problem "Express $z=\\frac{3}{1-\\text{i}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$ Give the **exact** values of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ in **radians** from $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(${{field-2}}$)+i\\operatorname{sin}⁡(${{field-3}}$))$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $1+\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{3}{1-\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1+\\text{i})}{(1-\\text{i})(1+\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1+\\text{i})}{{1}^{2}-{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1+\\text{i})}{1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1+\\text{i})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{9}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $y=\\frac{3}{2}$ are both positive, $z$ lies in the first quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\frac{3}{2}}{\\frac{3}{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266004 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a6128-22df-5a02-96fd-7c8cabf8f5f6" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Complex Number in Polar Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9102fc8-66b5-5764-959c-6d358d657262" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{146}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266006}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9102fc8-66b5-5764-959c-6d358d657262" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{146}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266006} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a9b5150-a9f6-58f0-bbd2-eba50485fcfb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266005} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4651e956-a7d3-5a0c-91ef-9421c4100c29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266008}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4651e956-a7d3-5a0c-91ef-9421c4100c29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266008} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfeb19ad-6ad8-5879-94e2-87865549622b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266007} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8c74103-c25c-584b-90a8-f8bbe35b8afd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266010}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8c74103-c25c-584b-90a8-f8bbe35b8afd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266010} :answer-field/id #uuid "f46f5889-a17e-5a81-b630-a85f8a045c6d" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266009}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ff3a2f18-7dab-5d55-9d4f-1b11e3d4584c" :question/math-academy-id "q-322670" :question/problem "Express $z=5-11\\text{i}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$ Give the **exact** value of $r$ and round $\\theta $ in **radians** from $[0,2\\pi )$ to **two decimal places**.\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(${{field-2}}$)+i\\operatorname{sin}⁡(${{field-3}}$))$" :question/worked-solution "In polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=5-11\\text{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+(-11)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+121} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{146}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=5$ is positive and $y=-11$ is negative, $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-11}{5}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -arctan\\,(\\frac{11}{5}) \\\\  & ≈5.14,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places. \n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z=5-11\\text{i}$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{146}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5.14)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5.14))\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266011 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a6128-22df-5a02-96fd-7c8cabf8f5f6" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Complex Number in Polar Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c67356ce-7c2c-5ea0-b7d7-c30c1897900f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266013} {:answer/id #uuid "6994327e-554f-5047-8669-377864db641e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266014}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6994327e-554f-5047-8669-377864db641e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266014} :answer-field/id #uuid "a99b96f3-30f8-5e7a-b729-8240c479729a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266012} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e518ec0d-10d6-5d05-84eb-da99797d902b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266016}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e518ec0d-10d6-5d05-84eb-da99797d902b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266016} :answer-field/id #uuid "801da17f-b817-5874-96d7-3440b4026507" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266015} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8001af7b-8610-54cc-9c1c-676bcb3e1f74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266018}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8001af7b-8610-54cc-9c1c-676bcb3e1f74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266018} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bc54cab-ae38-552f-a1af-c3c8cf6538e6" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266017}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "315a769c-4170-5163-b093-33a48aeaf82a" :question/math-academy-id "q-322671" :question/problem "Express $z=\\sqrt{3}-\\text{i}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$ Give the **exact** values of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ in **radians** from $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(${{field-2}}$)+\\,i\\operatorname{sin}⁡(${{field-3}}$))$" :question/worked-solution "In polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=\\sqrt{3}-\\text{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $ represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=\\sqrt{3}>0$ and $y=-1<0,$ $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{3}}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -arctan\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z=\\sqrt{3}-\\text{i}$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}))\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266019 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31e4455a-9f8f-5c12-8c96-bb6735691591" :knowledge-point/title "Converting From Polar Form to Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a9c5ca9-2e05-5d9c-9ae1-16f7e1e93a28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=-1+\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266021} {:answer/id #uuid "6f0a216b-686b-57e9-b222-f06ebd093205" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{3}-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266022} {:answer/id #uuid "6f7425a7-31fd-5ec4-8864-cf8c93312020" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266023} {:answer/id #uuid "b72c2978-72a1-5c2a-8a34-11f6cc61ed5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=1-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266024} {:answer/id #uuid "e4a28222-ca80-5e71-a22c-223f705e152d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=1-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266025}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4a28222-ca80-5e71-a22c-223f705e152d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=1-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266025} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c4fbd94-97e3-513a-a3cc-c18d1f9320c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266020}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "14bc3738-08e4-584c-8b05-fa64bde46f62" :question/math-academy-id "q-69967" :question/problem "Express $z=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}))$ in the form $z=x+\\text{i}y.$" :question/worked-solution "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nFirst, we find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}):$\n \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\n \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{3})=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the values and expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{2}-\\text{i}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266026 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a6128-22df-5a02-96fd-7c8cabf8f5f6" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Complex Number in Polar Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81f5da0c-510f-5e35-b0ee-287b70870625" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=13(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.176)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1.176))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266028} {:answer/id #uuid "eed5f1af-d029-5e1e-94f1-ca93d6cc81ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=13(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.641)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1.641))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266029} {:answer/id #uuid "45dd2f36-ec7d-5a79-b200-797ca20b4ab7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{15}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.486)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1.486))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266030} {:answer/id #uuid "1307e854-092c-561e-b48c-d3ed760d21fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{13}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.176)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1.176))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266031} {:answer/id #uuid "acbe0745-cd41-5df6-af32-46a37b60791d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=15(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.723)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1.723))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81f5da0c-510f-5e35-b0ee-287b70870625" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=13(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.176)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1.176))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266028} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef841a54-aec6-536f-8438-e9e88ec75019" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266027}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7039d956-de07-5ae4-acb5-d43443cbc317" :question/math-academy-id "q-8263" :question/problem "Express $z=5+12\\text{i}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$ Give $\\theta $ in radians to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "In polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=5+12\\text{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{12}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{169} \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=5$ is positive and $y=12$ is positive, $z$ lies in the first quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{12}{5}) \\\\  & ≈1.176,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. \n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z=5+12\\text{i}$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=13(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.176)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1.176))\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266033 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d1ea5c4-2700-5c03-a22e-de5439f47c36" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Quotient of Complex Numbers in Polar Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5be99101-da47-5b60-a7ff-1d4866ac49dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266035} {:answer/id #uuid "4931231f-8fe2-5d63-930a-9fad33a60565" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266036} {:answer/id #uuid "d395ff26-bd69-5d3e-a7a3-80b698bce481" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266037} {:answer/id #uuid "e383b42a-a1f0-518f-85ba-2169a6672380" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266038} {:answer/id #uuid "f6eebcb8-c6f5-5f89-b376-0b5ac080a01f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4931231f-8fe2-5d63-930a-9fad33a60565" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266036} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6bbc968-c9c1-56e3-a177-4ae86e3b8b16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266034}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a69f1b0-3405-5ab6-ad35-05b2ddc39c21" :question/math-academy-id "q-86605" :question/problem "Express $z=\\frac{2}{1+\\text{i}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $1-\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{2}{1+\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-\\text{i})}{(1+\\text{i})(1-\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-\\text{i})}{{1}^{2}-{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-\\text{i})}{1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-\\text{i})}{2} \\\\  & =1-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=1-\\text{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=1$ is positive and $y=-1$ is negative, $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-1}{1}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1 \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}))\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266040 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d1ea5c4-2700-5c03-a22e-de5439f47c36" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Quotient of Complex Numbers in Polar Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f850bdda-0b2b-549b-bb53-bc93ccbb9d89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5.640)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5.640)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266042} {:answer/id #uuid "3809dc0c-e1da-5a4a-a5c1-7603dd3a2e94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3.348)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3.348)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266043} {:answer/id #uuid "091da9c1-57f8-539d-a586-9bd526967168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4.234)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4.234))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266044} {:answer/id #uuid "c9db56d6-479f-50c5-aa63-c586f774158a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5.640)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5.640))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266045} {:answer/id #uuid "0dc38f84-814f-5d77-ac08-3cc5d500805e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4.234)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4.234)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f850bdda-0b2b-549b-bb53-bc93ccbb9d89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5.640)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5.640)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266042} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfd2208e-a3d0-5013-ab08-0adf2908cb77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266041}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9b4fd445-6363-58ec-83c1-a1c688bd3d69" :question/math-academy-id "q-86606" :question/problem "Express $z=\\frac{5}{4+3\\text{i}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$ Give $\\theta $ in radians to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $4-3\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{5}{4+3\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5(4-3\\text{i})}{(4+3\\text{i})(4-3\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5(4-3\\text{i})}{{4}^{2}-9{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5(4-3\\text{i})}{16+9} \\\\  & =\\frac{5(4-3\\text{i})}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{3}{5}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{3}{5}\\text{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{16}{25}+\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{16+9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{25}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $ represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=\\frac{4}{5}$ is positive and $y=-\\frac{3}{5}$ is negative, $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{(-\\frac{3}{5})}{(\\frac{4}{5})}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & ≈5.640,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. \n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5.640)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5.640)\n$$"}]}
